# Symposium

The end product, in the order you use it. Click **1** before you speak.

| | What it does |
|---|---|
| **1 · Check everything** | Services, props interlock, camera, radios, flight controller, phone URL. Green means go. |
| **2 · Phone camera feed** | The captive-portal address, for when the redirect is working. |
| **3 · Wi-Fi hotspot** | Switch between the venue's Wi-Fi and the drone hosting its own network. |
| **4 · Missions and motor test** | Jetson-side mission menu. The handheld remote is the better demo — this is the backup. |
| **5 · Camera** | **Use this one for the phone.** Opens the feed and prints every address that works. |

### Use 5, not 2, if you only remember one

Icon **5** leads with `http://10.42.0.1:8080/?k=…` — the port the server actually
listens on, which works whether or not the captive-portal redirect loaded. Icon
**2** gives `venatordrone.com`, which is prettier but needs an `nft` rule on top
of DNS; when that rule fails the phone just gets a dead connection with no clue
why.

### Never start a second camera

The OAK takes **one** host process, and `venator-gui` already has it. Running
`camera.sh preview` or `oak_camera.sh detect` while it is up gives
`X_LINK_DEVICE_ALREADY_IN_USE` — that is the arbitration working, not a fault.
To see the feed on this Jetson, click **5 · Camera**; it opens the stream that
is already running instead of competing for the device.

## What starts by itself

These three are **systemd services**. They come up a few seconds after power-on,
with no login and nothing clicked:

| Service | Does |
|---|---|
| `venator-gui` | The camera feed on port 8080 |
| `venator-net-fallback` | Hosts `VenatorDrone` when no known Wi-Fi is in range |
| `venator-c2` | The LoRa command link the handheld remote talks to |

Everything in this folder is a **tool you invoke** — none of them need to be
running for the demo to work.

## The demo

1. **1 · Check everything** — expect `ALL CLEAR`.
2. Phone joins `VenatorDrone`, opens the URL from **2**. Live video.
3. On the handheld remote, in this order:
   - **RUN 7** Drone status — changes nothing, proves the round trip.
   - **RUN 1** Test each motor — 1→4, three seconds each.
   - **RUN 2** Test all motors — all four together.
   - **RUN 8** Hover 3 ft — **it refuses**: `props are OFF`. Show this one; it
     is the best evidence the safety gate is real.

Props stay off. Battery connected (the ESCs need it or nothing turns, even
though the commands are accepted). Kill switch in hand.

## If asked

- **"Has it flown?"** Not outdoors. Motors have spun on the real aircraft,
  commanded from the handheld over LoRa with no network of any kind — that is
  the result, and it is true.
- **"What about GPS?"** `fix_type 0` — the M10 is fitted but not detected, a
  cable or port fault. It blocks outdoor flight and nothing being shown here.
- **Camera stutters?** It reconnects itself in about five seconds. Say so and
  keep going; do not replug anything mid-demo — disturbing the USB bus is what
  knocks it over.

The statement you are delivering: [`docs/MISSION.md`](__REPO__/docs/MISSION.md).
Engineering status, if anyone digs: [`docs/ROADMAP.md`](__REPO__/docs/ROADMAP.md).
